WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.388
Now we add a second grouping column The result grain
changes from

00:00:04.412 --> 00:00:09.750
one row per diagnosis to one row per
patient-and-diagnosis combination The SELECT

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:14.388
list contains subject_id and diagnosis and both
columns also appear in GROUP

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.562
BY COUNT star then tells us how many admission rows
exist for

00:00:18.588 --> 00:00:23.562
each specific combination Imagine that two different
patients both have the same

00:00:23.588 --> 00:00:28.812
diagnosis When we grouped only by diagnosis those
rows contributed to one

00:00:28.825 --> 00:00:34.425
shared diagnosis group After adding subject_id those
rows are separated

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:40.512
into different patient-level groups This is why
grouping columns define detail More

00:00:40.538 --> 00:00:44.962
grouping columns usually produce a more detailed
summary while fewer grouping columns

00:00:44.988 --> 00:00:49.625
produce a more aggregated summary Run the query and
compare it mentally

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:54.775
with the previous slide The source table did not
change Only the

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.238
analytical grain changed When building a report be
deliberate about this choice

00:01:01.112 --> 00:01:06.488
One row per diagnosis answers a population-level
question One row per patient

00:01:06.512 --> 00:01:12.762
and diagnosis answers a more detailed question
Neither is automatically better The

00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:16.250
correct grain depends on what the report is supposed
to represent
